WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.725
What's the structure underneath?

2
00:00:01.725 --> 00:00:04.350
Two parts: privilege and applications.

3
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.311
For privilege: use the least-privileged
built-in role for each task;

4
00:00:08.311 --> 00:00:12.488
keep fewer than five Global
Administrators, as Microsoft recommends;

5
00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:14.145
use role-assignable groups,

6
00:00:14.145 --> 00:00:18.898
which take assigned membership only and
are limited to five hundred per tenant;

7
00:00:18.898 --> 00:00:22.716
and tier everything along
Microsoft's enterprise access model,

8
00:00:22.716 --> 00:00:27.325
which builds on the old Active Directory
tier model rather than replacing it.

9
00:00:27.325 --> 00:00:31.587
For applications: application
owners actually hold administrator

10
00:00:31.587 --> 00:00:35.850
rights over their app, including
adding a credential, so keep them few.

11
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.587
Admin consent goes through
a governed workflow.

12
00:00:38.587 --> 00:00:42.896
Applications authenticate with
certificates or federated credentials

13
00:00:42.896 --> 00:00:46.649
rather than secrets, and Azure
workloads use managed identities.

14
00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:50.020
What people get wrong:
consent grants never expire,

15
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.550
and revoking one leaves
already-issued tokens valid.

16
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.500
So application access needs the
same review discipline as people.
